1. Artifact

An artifact is the output/package produced by your build process.

Example for a Java Maven project:

Source code
    ↓
   Maven
    ↓
target/
    ├── app.jar
    └── app.war

app.jar or app.war is an artifact.

Other examples:

Java → .jar, .war
Python → .whl
Docker → Docker image
Android → .apk


2. Artifact Repository

An artifact repository is a place/server where those artifacts are stored and managed.

Examples:

Nexus Repository
JFrog Artifactory
AWS CodeArtifact

Think:

Developer
    ↓
Source Code
    ↓
Maven Build
    ↓
app.jar          ← Artifact
    ↓
Upload
    ↓
Nexus            ← Artifact Repository


app.jar	Nexus
app.war	JFrog Artifactory

---------------------------------------------
Library

A library is reusable code that another program can use.

Example:

Your Java application
       ↓
   Spring library
       ↓
   Reusable Java code

A library provides functionality so you don't have to write everything yourself.

Examples:

Spring
JUnit
Jackson
NumPy
React
Yes — spring-core is a library. But someone has to create that library first.

How spring-core is created

The Spring developers write Java source code:

Spring developers
      ↓
Java source code
      ↓
Compile
      ↓
.class files
      ↓
Package
      ↓
spring-core-6.2.0.jar

That .jar file is the packaged form of the spring-core library.

Then how does Maven get it?

The Spring team publishes the JAR to a Maven repository:

Spring developers
       ↓
create spring-core
       ↓
spring-core-6.2.0.jar
       ↓
Maven Repository
       ↓
Your project
       ↓
Maven downloads it

Your pom.xml tells Maven exactly which one you want:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-core</artifactId>
    <version>6.2.0</version>
</dependency>

So these are different things:

spring-core
     ↓
Library name


spring-core-6.2.0.jar
     ↓
Packaged library/artifact


pom.xml
     ↓
Your project says:
"I depend on this library"


Maven
     ↓
Downloads it from a repository

In short: Developers create the library → package it as a JAR → publish it to a Maven repository → your Maven project downloads it as a dependency.

A package manager is a tool that installs, updates, removes, and manages software packages and their dependencies.
Package

A package is a distributable unit that contains software/code and usually metadata.

For example, a Maven package/artifact might be:

spring-core-6.2.0.jar

That .jar contains compiled Java classes and other resources.

Simple relationship

Think of it like this:

Library = reusable code


Package = a packaged/distributable form of that code

For example:

Spring library
      ↓
packaged as
      ↓
spring-core.jar
      ↓
stored in
      ↓
Maven Repository
But one important point

The word package can mean different things depending on the technology.



Term	Meaning
Java library	Reusable Java code
Maven package/artifact	Distributable Java artifact such as .jar
Python package	Collection of Python modules
APT package	Linux software package such as nginx
npm package	JavaScript package distributed through npm

So don't treat library = package in every situation.

Easy way to remember:

Library = the reusable functionality.
Package = how software/code is organized and distributed.


Maven is primarily a Build Tool, but it also does dependency/package management.

So the best answer is:

Maven = Build Tool + Dependency Management Tool

What Maven does
pom.xml
   ↓
 Maven
   ├── Downloads dependencies → dependency management
   ├── Compiles code
   ├── Runs tests
   ├── Packages application
   └── Creates JAR/WAR → build
-----------------------------------------------------

Build script

A build script contains instructions/commands that tell the build tool how to build the project.

Example:

build.sh
javac *.java
jar -cf app.jar *.class

It says what actions to perform.

Build configuration file

A build configuration file mainly contains settings/information for the build tool.

Example:

pom.xml

It tells Maven things like:

Project name/version
Dependencies
Java version
Plugins
Packaging type
Build settings
But pom.xml can do both?

pom.xml is primarily a build configuration file, not a traditional build script.

pom.xml
   ↓
Maven reads configuration
   ↓
Maven performs build
   ↓
target/app.jar

Similarly, setup.py can contain configuration plus executable Python build/package logic, depending on how it's used.

Easy distinction

Build script = instructions/commands to execute.
Build configuration = settings that tell the build tool how the project should be built.

So when someone says "build script/config file", they are grouping two related things together, but they are not exactly the same.
-------------------------------------------------------------

When you say:

CI/CD pipeline runs Maven to install dependencies

Maven looks at your pom.xml to know which dependencies your project needs.

For example:

<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-core</artifactId>
        <version>...</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>

Then when the pipeline runs:

mvn package

Maven:

pom.xml
   ↓
Read dependencies
   ↓
Download required libraries
   ↓
Compile code
   ↓
Run tests
   ↓
Create artifact
   ↓
target/app.jar

So:

pom.xml → declares what dependencies are needed

Maven → downloads/manages those dependencies and performs the build
For npm, the equivalent is usually:

package.json
   ↓
npm install
   ↓
node_modules/

---------------------------------------------------

Each build/package tool has its own configuration file:

Tool	Configuration file	Dependencies
Maven	pom.xml	Java libraries
npm	package.json	JavaScript/Node.js packages
Python pip	requirements.txt or pyproject.toml	Python packages

Maven
mvn package
    ↓
looks at pom.xml
    ↓
finds dependencies
    ↓
downloads them
npm
npm install
    ↓
looks at package.json
    ↓
finds dependencies
    ↓
downloads them
    ↓
node_modules/

So remember:

Maven → pom.xml
npm → package.json

Build tools to learn for DevOps
1. Maven — Java ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

You should learn this first because Java applications are very common in DevOps.

Learn:

pom.xml
   ↓
Dependencies
   ↓
mvn clean
mvn compile
mvn test
mvn package
mvn install

Understand:

pom.xml
Dependencies
Maven lifecycle
target/
JAR/WAR artifacts
Maven repositories
Maven Central
Nexus/Artifactory
Running Maven inside Jenkins

Example:

mvn clean package
2. npm — JavaScript/React/Node.js ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Learn:

package.json
package-lock.json
node_modules/
npm install
npm test
npm run build

Understand:

npm install
npm run build

For a React application:

Source code
    ↓
npm install
    ↓
Dependencies
    ↓
npm run build
    ↓
build/dist
    ↓
Docker image
3. Gradle — Java ⭐⭐⭐

You don't need to go extremely deep initially.

Learn:

build.gradle
gradlew
gradle build
gradle test
gradle clean

Understand that Gradle can perform a similar role to Maven for Java projects.

4. Python build/package tools ⭐⭐

For DevOps, basic knowledge is enough initially.

Learn:

requirements.txt
pyproject.toml
pip
pip install

Example:

pip install -r requirements.txt
Most important for you

Since you're learning DevOps, I'd follow this order:

1. Maven
     ↓
2. npm
     ↓
3. Gradle
     ↓
4. Python pip/pyproject

Then learn how they fit into a CI/CD pipeline:

GitHub
   ↓
Jenkins
   ↓
Maven / npm
   ↓
Build + Test
   ↓
Artifact
   ↓
Nexus / Docker Registry
   ↓
Docker
   ↓
Kubernetes / AWS
What you should be able to explain in an interview

For example:

"Jenkins checks out the source code from GitHub. Maven reads the pom.xml, downloads the required dependencies, compiles the source code, runs tests,
and packages the application into a JAR/WAR artifact. The artifact can then be stored in Nexus or used to build a Docker image."

---------------------------------------
1. Library = reusable code

A library is code written by someone else that you use in your application.

Example:

Spring
   ↓
Java library
   ↓
Provides reusable classes/functions

You don't need to write that functionality yourself.

2. Package = distributable unit

A package is a way of bundling and distributing software/code.

For Java:

Spring library
      ↓
spring-core.jar
      ↓
Package/artifact

For JavaScript:

React library
      ↓
npm package
      ↓
react

So a library can be distributed as a package.

3. Dependency = something your application needs

A dependency describes a relationship:

"My application depends on this library/package."

For example:

Your Java application
       ↓
depends on
       ↓
Spring library

Therefore, Spring is a dependency of your application.

In Maven:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-core</artifactId>
    <version>...</version>
</dependency>

This tells Maven:

"My application needs this dependency."

Very simple example
Library
  ↓
Spring


Package
  ↓
spring-core.jar


Dependency
  ↓
Your application needs Spring

So remember:

Library = reusable code
Package = how code/software is bundled and distributed
Dependency = something your application needs to work

----------------------------------------------------------




mvn deploy is used to build the project and upload the generated artifact to a remote Maven repository such as Nexus or Artifactory.

Maven lifecycle

The commands you asked about fit together like this:

mvn clean
    ↓
Deletes target/


mvn compile
    ↓
Compiles source code


mvn test
    ↓
Runs tests


mvn package
    ↓
Creates JAR/WAR in target/


mvn install
    ↓
Copies artifact to your LOCAL Maven repository


mvn deploy
    ↓
Uploads artifact to REMOTE Maven repository
mvn install vs mvn deploy

This is the important difference:

mvn install
     ↓
app.jar
     ↓
Local repository
~/.m2/repository/

Whereas:

mvn deploy
     ↓
app.jar
     ↓
Remote repository
Nexus / Artifactory

For example, in a CI/CD pipeline:

GitHub
   ↓
Jenkins
   ↓
mvn clean package
   ↓
app.jar
   ↓
mvn deploy
   ↓
Nexus Repository

So remember:

mvn install → stores the artifact locally
mvn deploy → uploads the artifact to a remote Maven repository

mvn compile compiles your Java source code into .class files.

Example:

src/main/java/
    App.java
       ↓
   mvn compile
       ↓
target/classes/
    App.class


mvn compile → .class files
mvn test    → run tests
mvn package → create .jar/.war

mvn package builds your Java application and creates the final artifact, usually a .jar or .war file.

Example:

src/
  ↓
Java source code
  ↓
mvn package
  ↓
compile
  ↓
run tests
  ↓
create JAR/WAR
  ↓
target/
   └── app.jar

So:

mvn package = build/package the application → create the JAR/WAR in target/.

Then you can upload that JAR/WAR to an artifact repository such as Nexus using mvn deploy.

There isn't actually a second separate compile.

When you run:

mvn package

Maven follows its lifecycle and includes the compile phase automatically.

So:

mvn compile

means:

Stop after compiling.

But:

mvn package

means:

Go through the earlier phases too, including compile, test, and then package.

For example:

mvn package
   ↓
validate
   ↓
compile       ← compilation happens here
   ↓
test
   ↓
package       ← creates JAR/WAR

So if you already ran:

mvn compile

and then run:

mvn package

Maven doesn't normally need to compile everything from scratch; it can reuse what's already built if nothing relevant changed.

Key point: package is a later phase that includes the earlier phases.

without running compile we can run directly package'

Yes, exactly. You can directly run:

mvn package

You do not need to run mvn compile first.

Because package automatically goes through the earlier Maven lifecycle phases:

mvn package
   ↓
compile
   ↓
test
   ↓
package
   ↓
target/app.jar

So:

mvn compile

means only compile.

mvn package

means compile + test + package.

And commonly:

mvn clean package

means:

delete old target/
      ↓
compile
      ↓
test
      ↓
create new JAR/WAR

So in a CI/CD pipeline, you can normally use mvn clean package directly.

if you mean mvn package, that's basically correct.

mvn package
    ↓
1. Read pom.xml
    ↓
2. Download required dependencies
    ↓
3. Compile Java code
    ↓
4. Run tests
    ↓
5. Create .jar / .war
    ↓
target/app.jar

So you can remember:

mvn package = get dependencies + compile + test + create the JAR/WAR artifact.

One small correction: Maven doesn't always download dependencies every time. If they are already available in the local Maven repository (~/.m2), Maven can reuse them.
“Get dependencies” means Maven gets the libraries your application needs.

For example, your pom.xml says:

Your application
      ↓
needs Spring library
      ↓
pom.xml mentions Spring
      ↓
Maven gets/downloads Spring
      ↓
Now your code can use Spring

So:

Dependency = library that your application needs.

And “get dependencies” = Maven finds and makes those required libraries available to your project.

Dependencies means:

Libraries that your application needs to run or build.

Example:

Your Java application
        ↓
needs Spring library
        ↓
Spring = dependency

In pom.xml:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-core</artifactId>
    <version>6.2.0</version>
</dependency>

Here Spring Core is a dependency of your application, and 6.2.0 specifies which version Maven should use.

So simply:

Dependency = a library/package that your project depends on.
